Export the UNICODE flavour and detect mismatches

On Windows StormLib takes every local file name as a TCHAR string, and
STORM_UNICODE only adds UNICODE and _UNICODE to the library's own PRIVATE
compile definitions. A consumer compiles against StormLib.h with whatever
UNICODE setting it happens to have, so a UNICODE StormLib used from an ANSI
program -- or the reverse -- compiles and links cleanly, then hands narrow
strings to wide parameters at run time. Nothing in the header can tell.

CascLib, by the same author, already solves this: its CMake target exports
CASCLIB_UNICODE for a UNICODE build and CASCLIB_DETECT_UNICODE_MISMATCHES on
Windows, and CascLib.h stops with #error when the consumer disagrees. Do the
same here with STORMLIB_UNICODE and STORMLIB_DETECT_UNICODE_MISMATCHES. The
check requires UNICODE and _UNICODE to both match: winnt.h picks the width of
TCHAR and LPCTSTR from UNICODE, while StormPort.h and <tchar.h> look at
_UNICODE as well.

Both definitions are INTERFACE-only, so the library's own sources, which
include StormLib.h too, are not affected. Consumers that bypass the CMake
package get no check; StormLib ships no pkg-config file, so CMake is the
supported way in.

Upstream: not submitted yet
Applies-to: all environments (the definitions only exist on Windows)

--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -359,9 +359,11 @@
     if(STORM_UNICODE)
         message(STATUS "Build UNICODE version")
         target_compile_definitions(${LIBRARY_NAME} PRIVATE UNICODE _UNICODE)
+        target_compile_definitions(${LIBRARY_NAME} INTERFACE STORMLIB_UNICODE)
     else()
         message(STATUS "Build ANSI version")
     endif()
+    target_compile_definitions(${LIBRARY_NAME} INTERFACE STORMLIB_DETECT_UNICODE_MISMATCHES)
 endif()
 
 target_link_libraries(${LIBRARY_NAME} PRIVATE ${LINK_LIBS})
--- a/src/StormLib.h
+++ b/src/StormLib.h
@@ -88,6 +88,22 @@
 
 #include "StormPort.h"
 
+//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// The consumer has to use the UNICODE setting the library was built with
+//
+// On Windows every local file name crosses this API as a TCHAR string, and
+// the width of TCHAR follows the UNICODE setting of whoever includes this
+// header, not the one the library was compiled with. The CMake package
+// exports STORMLIB_DETECT_UNICODE_MISMATCHES on Windows, and STORMLIB_UNICODE
+// for a UNICODE build, so that a mismatch stops the compile instead of
+// handing narrow strings to wide parameters, or the reverse, at run time.
+
+#if defined(STORMLIB_DETECT_UNICODE_MISMATCHES)
+#if defined(UNICODE) != defined(STORMLIB_UNICODE) || defined(_UNICODE) != defined(STORMLIB_UNICODE)
+#error StormLib was not built with the same UNICODE setting as your project
+#endif
+#endif
+
 #ifdef __cplusplus
 extern "C" {
 #endif
